/* Original teaching text and fictional examples; no private or production data. */
(function(root){
const lessons=[];let group='01 · Understanding data';
function add(id,title,intro,words,formula,deeper,examples,mistake,summary){lessons.push({id,title,group,intro,words:words.split('|'),formula,deeper,examples:examples.map(([q,steps,result])=>({q,steps:steps.split('|'),result})),mistake,summary});}
add('population','Population and sample: who are we studying?',
'Statistics helps us learn from data while being honest about uncertainty. Imagine wanting to know the average travel time of every student in a school. The whole school is your population. The students you actually ask form your sample. A summary of that sample describes the people you asked; extending it to everyone requires a suitable sampling process.',
'Population = the whole group of interest.|Sample = the observations you actually collect.|Parameter = a population quantity; statistic = a quantity calculated from a sample.',
'Sample fraction = sample size / population size',
'Descriptive statistics summarize observed data. Inferential statistics use observations to reason about a population or process. First define the population, observation unit, time period and question. A census tries to measure every unit, but can still have measurement or coverage errors. A larger sample reduces some random uncertainty; it does not automatically repair an unrepresentative selection.',
[['Identify the groups','A school has 500 students.|You ask 50 students about travel time.|The population is 500 students and the sample is those 50.','The sampling fraction is 50/500=10%. This alone does not establish representativeness.'],['Calculate a sample summary','Three sampled travel times are 10,20 and 30 minutes.|Add them: 60 minutes.|Divide by 3: sample mean=20 minutes.','20 describes this sample. It is an estimate, not proof, of the school’s mean.'],['Change the target population','A survey covers daytime students only.|The question asks about all daytime and evening students.|Evening students are absent from the sampling frame.','The survey may not support the intended all-student conclusion.']],
'Do not confuse the group observed with the group you want to describe.','Always ask: who is the population, and how did these observations enter the sample?');
add('types','Data types: labels, counts and measurements',
'Different data answer different questions. A color is a label, the number of books is a count, and a measured height is a numerical measurement. Before calculating anything, identify what each column means. A numeric-looking code may still be only a label.',
'Categorical = values name groups.|Discrete = separate countable values.|Continuous = measurements modeled on a continuum, even when recorded with rounding.',
'One row = one defined observation; one column = one defined variable',
'Nominal categories have no inherent order; ordinal categories do. Numerical variables support arithmetic when their units and meaning justify it. An identifier such as a postal code is not a quantity to average. A count can have a maximum, such as correct answers out of ten, which influences the probability model you choose.',
[['Classify three columns','Favorite color names a category.|Number of siblings is a discrete count.|Height in centimeters is a continuous measurement.','Use category counts for color and suitable numeric summaries for counts or heights.'],['Spot a misleading number','Students have ID codes 101,102 and 103.|Their arithmetic average is 102.|That does not make student 102 an “average student.”','IDs are labels, despite being written with digits.'],['Recognize ordered categories','Ratings are poor, fair, good and excellent.|They have a meaningful order.|The distance between adjacent labels is not automatically equal.','A median category can make sense; numeric spacing needs an explicit assumption.']],
'Do not choose a method solely from how a column is stored in software.','The meaning of the values determines which calculations are sensible.');
add('measurement','Measurement scales, units and precision',
'A measurement needs a unit and a definition. A value of 5 could mean minutes, meters or items. Precision describes how finely you record a value; accuracy concerns closeness to the intended truth. Repeating the same biased measurement can be precise but inaccurate.',
'Unit = the scale attached to a measurement.|Precision = fineness or repeatability of measurement, depending on context.|Accuracy = closeness to the target truth.',
'Converted value = original value × conversion factor',
'Nominal scales label groups; ordinal scales rank them; interval scales have meaningful differences; ratio scales also have a meaningful zero. Celsius temperature has meaningful differences but a Celsius ratio does not describe “twice as hot.” Document measurement procedures, rounding and calibration so that apparent statistical differences are not just instrument differences.',
[['Convert before combining','One length is 1 meter and another is 50 centimeters.|Convert 1 meter to 100 centimeters.|Average (100+50)/2=75 centimeters.','Averaging 1 and 50 directly would mix incompatible units.'],['Separate rounding from reality','A scale displays 12.3 kilograms.|The last shown decimal is 0.1 kilogram.|This does not prove the true value is exactly 12.3000.','Display precision alone does not establish accuracy.'],['Check ratios','Temperatures are 10°C and 20°C.|Their difference is 10°C.|20°C is not physically twice the temperature of 10°C on an absolute scale.','Ratios require a meaningful zero for the quantity being compared.']],
'Do not report more meaningful digits than the measurement process supports.','Define the quantity, unit and measurement procedure before comparing numbers.');
add('collection','Collecting data and defining one observation',
'A dataset is a record of a process. You need to know what one row represents before counting or averaging rows. Ten purchases by one customer are ten transactions but only one customer. The correct unit depends on the question.',
'Observation unit = the thing each record describes.|Observational study = researchers observe existing conditions.|Experiment = researchers assign conditions or treatments.',
'Rate = number of events / relevant exposure',
'Define eligibility, time window, inclusion rules and collection method before analysis. Repeated records from the same unit are often dependent. A rate needs the correct exposure, such as machine-hours rather than just machines. Random assignment in an experiment supports a different kind of inference from simply observing which people choose a treatment.',
[['Count customers correctly','A table has 8 purchases by Ada and 2 by Ben.|There are 10 purchase rows.|There are only 2 distinct customers.','Transaction-level and customer-level averages answer different questions.'],['Use the right exposure','Machine A has 4 events in 20 hours.|Machine B has 6 events in 60 hours.|Rates are 4/20=0.2 and 6/60=0.1 per hour.','B has more events but a lower event rate.'],['Distinguish observation from assignment','People choose whether to attend a workshop.|You compare attendees with non-attendees.|Their motivation may differ before the workshop.','An observed difference is not automatically caused by attendance.']],
'Do not treat duplicated or repeated rows as independent new people.','Know the unit, time window and exposure behind every row.');
add('missing','Missing data, outliers and data checks',
'A blank value is not the same as zero. A strange value is not automatically wrong. Start by checking units, duplicates, impossible values and how information became missing. Decisions about cleaning can change the result, so record them.',
'Missing value = a value was not observed or recorded.|Outlier = an observation unusual relative to a specified comparison.|Imputation = filling a missing value using an explicit method.',
'Observed mean = sum of observed values / number observed',
'Missing completely at random means missingness is unrelated to observed and unobserved values. Missing at random means it can depend on observed information after conditioning appropriately. Missing not at random can depend on the unobserved value itself. These are assumptions about the missingness process, not facts established by a blank-cell count. Mean filling understates variability unless the inferential method accounts for imputation.',
[['Do not turn blanks into zero','Recorded values are 10,20 and missing.|Observed mean=(10+20)/2=15.|Replacing the blank by 0 gives 30/3=10.','An unsupported replacement changes the estimate.'],['Investigate a large value','Daily counts are 3,4,4 and 40.|Check whether 40 is a typo, a special day or a different unit.|If it is genuine, retaining and explaining it may be appropriate.','Do not delete an observation merely because it changes the conclusion.'],['Check missingness','High-volume shops fail to return a survey more often.|Responding shops average 50 customers per day.|The missing high-volume shops may make that average too low for all shops.','Ignoring missingness can introduce selection bias.']],
'Automatic deletion or filling without a reason can hide an important pattern.','Cleaning is part of analysis: preserve meaning and document choices.');
group='02 · Summarizing values';
add('frequency','Frequency tables, proportions and percentages',
'A frequency table counts how often each value or category occurs. A proportion divides a count by the relevant total. A percentage is that proportion multiplied by 100. These simple summaries are often the clearest place to start.',
'Frequency = number of occurrences.|Proportion = part divided by whole.|Percentage point = the difference between two percentages.',
'Proportion = count / total; percentage = 100 × proportion',
'Always name the denominator. A percentage of all respondents differs from a percentage of respondents who answered one question. In a mutually exclusive, exhaustive category table, counts sum to the total and proportions sum to one. Multiple-response questions can legitimately sum to more than 100%. Relative percentage change and percentage-point change are different quantities.',
[['Count colors','Responses are red, blue, red, green, red.|Red occurs 3 times out of 5.|3/5=0.6, or 60%.','Frequency 3 and proportion 60% describe the same category.'],['Compare percentages','A rate rises from 20% to 30%.|The absolute change is 30−20=10 percentage points.|Relative change is (30−20)/20=50%.','“10 points” and “50% increase” are both correct but mean different things.'],['Check multiple selections','Ten people may select both tea and coffee.|Eight select tea and six coffee.|The percentages sum to 140% because selections overlap.','Percentages exceeding 100% are not always an error.']],
'Never report a percentage without making its denominator clear.','Counts describe how many; proportions describe the share of a defined whole.');
add('mean','The mean and weighted averages',
'The mean spreads the total equally across observations. If three children have 2,4 and 6 counters, an equal redistribution gives each 4. A weighted mean gives some observations more influence, but the reason for the weights must be clear.',
'Mean = sum divided by count.|Weight = relative contribution assigned to a value.|Weighted mean = weighted sum divided by total weight.',
'x̄ = Σxᵢ/n; weighted mean = Σwᵢxᵢ / Σwᵢ',
'The mean uses every value and therefore responds strongly to extreme values. Averaging group means without group sizes can be misleading. When combining groups, weight their means by their observation counts if the goal is the overall individual-level mean. Survey weights may instead reflect selection probabilities. Distinguish those interpretations.',
[['Redistribute a total','Values are 2,4 and 6.|Their sum is 12.|12/3=4.','The mean is 4, even though equal redistribution is only an interpretation.'],['Combine unequal groups','Two observations average 10; eight average 20.|Combined sum=2×10+8×20=180.|Combined mean=180/10=18.','A simple mean of the two group means, 15, would be wrong here.'],['Apply a course weight','Quiz score 80 has weight 1; project score 60 has weight 3.|Weighted sum=80+180=260.|Total weight=4; mean=65.','The project contributes three times as much weight.']],
'Do not average rates or group means without checking the appropriate weights.','The mean is a balance point for the total, and weights determine whose values count more.');
add('median','Median, mode and resistant summaries',
'The median is the middle of an ordered list. The mode is the most frequent value. The median is less sensitive than the mean to one very large value, making it useful for skewed quantities such as waiting times. No single summary is best for every purpose.',
'Median = middle ordered value, or average of the two middle values in an even numeric sample.|Mode = most frequent value.|Resistant = not changed drastically by a small number of extreme observations.',
'Sort first; locate the middle position(s)',
'The median depends primarily on order, not the size of the extremes. A sample may have several modes or no uniquely most frequent value. For categories, a mode can be meaningful when a numeric mean is not. A trimmed mean removes a stated proportion from both tails before averaging; trimming needs a transparent rule, not a choice made to produce a desired answer.',
[['Odd sample','Sort [7,1,3] into [1,3,7].|The second value is in the middle.|Median=3.','The mean is 11/3, so the two summaries need not match.'],['Even sample','Sort [2,4,6,20].|The middle two are 4 and 6.|Median=(4+6)/2=5.','Changing 20 to 200 leaves this median at 5.'],['Find a mode','Labels are A,A,B,C,C,C.|A occurs twice, B once and C three times.|The most frequent category is C.','Mode=C; averaging letter labels is not meaningful.']],
'Sorting is essential before selecting a median.','Use the median for a middle position and the mode for the most common value.');
add('quantiles','Quartiles, percentiles and the middle half',
'A percentile describes position in an ordered distribution. Quartiles divide the distribution into four parts. The first and third quartiles help summarize the middle half of values. Small-sample calculation conventions differ, so software can give slightly different quartiles without an error.',
'Q1 = first quartile.|Q3 = third quartile.|IQR = interquartile range, Q3 minus Q1.',
'IQR = Q3 − Q1',
'A percentile is not a percentage of the maximum value. For numerical examples here, when explicitly using the median-of-halves method on an even sample, split the sorted values into equal halves and take each half’s median. Other interpolation definitions are valid if reported consistently. Percentiles are useful for comparing relative positions and summarizing skewed data.',
[['Compute quartiles with a stated rule','Use [1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8].|Median-of-halves gives Q1=(2+3)/2=2.5 and Q3=(6+7)/2=6.5.|IQR=6.5−2.5=4.','The middle-half spread under this convention is 4.'],['Interpret a percentile','A score is at a reported 90th percentile.|It is high relative to the reference distribution.|It does not mean 90% of test questions were answered correctly.','Percentile rank and percentage score are different.'],['Use a supplied summary','Q1=10 and Q3=18.|Subtract 10 from 18.|IQR=8.','The IQR measures the width of the central half, not the full range.']],
'Always state or preserve the quantile convention when reproducing results.','Percentiles locate a value; the IQR summarizes the spread of the middle half.');
add('spread','Range, variance and standard deviation',
'Two groups can have the same average but very different variability. The range uses only the extremes. Variance averages squared distances from the mean under a specified convention. Standard deviation takes a square root so the result returns to the original unit.',
'Range = maximum minus minimum.|Population variance = average squared deviation over the population.|Sample variance = squared-deviation sum divided by n−1 for the usual unbiased variance estimator.',
'Population variance = Σ(xᵢ−μ)²/N; sample variance s² = Σ(xᵢ−x̄)²/(n−1)',
'The n−1 denominator corrects the usual sample variance’s bias under independent identically distributed sampling with finite variance. It does not make the sample standard deviation exactly unbiased. A population description and a sample-based estimate answer different questions. Standard deviation summarizes spread around the mean but can be strongly influenced by outliers.',
[['Describe a complete tiny population','Values are 2 and 4; mean=3.|Squared deviations are 1 and 1.|Population variance=(1+1)/2=1; standard deviation=1.','Range=2; population standard deviation=1.'],['Treat the same values as a sample','Squared-deviation sum remains 2.|Sample denominator is n−1=1.|Sample variance=2; sample standard deviation=√2≈1.4142.','The different denominator reflects a different inferential task.'],['Add the same constant','Add 10 to every value, producing 12 and 14.|Mean shifts from 3 to 13.|Distances from the mean remain −1 and 1.','Adding a constant changes the mean but not variance.']],
'Do not interchange variance and standard deviation: they have different units.','Choose the denominator deliberately, then distinguish squared spread from spread in original units.');
group='03 · Seeing patterns';
add('histogram','Histograms and the shape of data',
'A histogram groups numerical values into intervals and counts how many fall in each. It helps reveal clusters, gaps and long tails. A bar chart instead compares named categories. The way you choose histogram intervals affects what you see.',
'Bin = an interval used to group values.|Skew = asymmetry with a longer tail on one side.|Density histogram = a histogram whose total bar area is one.',
'Density height = bin count / (total count × bin width)',
'With equal-width bins, heights can show counts. With unequal-width bins, area rather than raw height must represent frequency for a fair density comparison. A right-skewed distribution has a long right tail, often pulling the mean above the median. Check several sensible bin widths; a single visual setting can hide or exaggerate apparent structure.',
[['Count into intervals','Values are 1,2,2,4,5.|Use bins [0,3) and [3,6).|Counts are 3 and 2.','The boundary convention makes the bins nonoverlapping.'],['Compare unequal widths','One bin has 4 of 10 observations and width 2.|Density height=4/(10×2)=0.2.|Area=0.2×2=0.4.','The bar area represents 40% of observations.'],['Read a long tail','Most waiting times are 2–5 minutes; one is 40.|The unusually long wait stretches the right tail.|The mean will be sensitive to that value.','Describe the tail instead of calling the whole distribution “normal.”']],
'Do not compare raw heights across unequal-width bins as if they were probabilities.','Histograms show distribution shape, but bin choices and axes matter.');
add('boxplot','Box plots and unusual observations',
'A box plot displays a median and quartiles compactly. Many versions use whiskers based on 1.5 times the IQR and draw farther observations separately. Those marked points deserve investigation, not automatic deletion.',
'Box = the interval from Q1 to Q3.|Fence = a threshold derived from the IQR.|Whisker = usually the farthest observed value inside a fence, depending on convention.',
'Lower fence = Q1−1.5IQR; upper fence = Q3+1.5IQR',
'A box plot hides detail: two different distributions can have the same five-number summary. Combine it with raw points or a histogram when possible. Fence flags are descriptive heuristics, not formal proof that a value is erroneous. State the quartile and whisker convention used by software.',
[['Calculate fences','Q1=10 and Q3=18, so IQR=8.|1.5×8=12.|Fences are 10−12=−2 and 18+12=30.','An observed 35 would be flagged by this rule.'],['Locate a whisker','The upper fence is 30; observed upper values are 22,28,35.|The largest observed value within the fence is 28.|35 is drawn separately.','The whisker ends at 28, not necessarily at the fence 30.'],['Compare spread','Group A has IQR 2 and group B has IQR 10.|B’s central half spans a wider interval.|Their medians could still be identical.','A box plot compares center and spread separately.']],
'A flagged point is not automatically a mistake or evidence of a different population.','Use box plots as compact summaries and investigate the observations behind them.');
add('scatter','Scatter plots: two measurements together',
'A scatter plot places one measurement horizontally and another vertically for each observation. It reveals direction, curvature, clusters and unusual pairs. Plotting paired values helps prevent a single summary from hiding the pattern.',
'Pair = two measurements from the same observation.|Association = a pattern linking variables.|Confounding = another variable can help explain an observed relationship.',
'One plotted point = (xᵢ,yᵢ) for the same unit',
'Do not sort the two columns independently: that destroys the original pairs. Use clear units and consider overplotting when many points overlap. A curved pattern may have little linear correlation despite a strong relationship. Observational association does not itself identify a causal mechanism.',
[['Plot three pairs','Pairs are (1,2),(2,4),(3,6).|As x increases, y increases proportionally.|The points lie on y=2x.','The pattern is perfectly increasing and linear.'],['Notice curvature','Pairs are (−1,1),(0,0),(1,1).|Both ends have a higher y than the middle.|The relationship is U-shaped, not a straight slope.','A straight-line summary can miss this pattern.'],['Preserve the pairings','Ada has x=1,y=10; Ben has x=2,y=5.|Sorting y alone gives 5,10.|That would incorrectly pair Ada with 5 and Ben with 10.','Keep values attached to the observation that produced them.']],
'A visually convincing association is not a randomized experiment.','Plot paired observations before reducing their relationship to one number.');
add('correlation','Covariance and correlation',
'Covariance describes whether two quantities tend to be above or below their means together. Correlation rescales this linear association to a unit-free value between −1 and 1 when both variables have positive variance. It measures linear movement, not every possible relationship.',
'Covariance = average joint centered movement under a chosen convention.|Correlation = covariance divided by both standard deviations.|r = sample Pearson correlation.',
'r = Σ(x−x̄)(y−ȳ) / √[Σ(x−x̄)² Σ(y−ȳ)²]',
'Correlation is unchanged by positive unit conversions but reverses sign if one variable is multiplied by a negative number. A constant variable has zero standard deviation, making correlation undefined. Outliers can dominate r. Restricted ranges and mixed groups can also change its interpretation.',
[['A perfect increasing line','x=[1,2,3], y=[2,4,6].|Centered values are [−1,0,1] and [−2,0,2].|Numerator=4; denominator=√(2×8)=4.','r=1.'],['A perfect decreasing line','x=[1,2,3], y=[6,4,2].|Centered products sum to −4.|The denominator remains 4.','r=−1.'],['Zero linear correlation can hide dependence','x=[−1,0,1], y=[1,0,1].|Symmetric positive and negative centered products cancel.|Yet y is exactly x².','Zero correlation does not imply independence.']],
'Correlation cannot establish that changing x will cause y to change.','Correlation summarizes linear association after removing units.');
add('transform','Standardization, transformations and misleading charts',
'Transformations change the scale on which you view data. Standardization expresses distance from a mean in standard-deviation units. Logarithms can make multiplicative differences easier to compare. Any transformation should preserve an understandable interpretation of the question.',
'z-score = distance from the mean divided by standard deviation.|Log scale = equal steps represent equal ratios.|Truncated axis = an axis that omits part of the scale.',
'z=(x−mean)/standard deviation, when standard deviation>0',
'Standardizing does not make a distribution normal. A log of zero or a negative number is not defined on the ordinary real log scale. An arbitrary offset before logging changes the interpretation and needs justification. Axis choices, area encodings and unequal time spacing can exaggerate a visual effect even with correct data.',
[['Compute a z-score','A score is 70, mean is 50 and standard deviation is 10.|Distance from mean=20.|z=20/10=2.','The score lies two standard deviations above the mean.'],['Read a log scale','Values are 1,10 and 100.|Their base-10 logs are 0,1 and 2.|Each equal log step means multiplying by 10.','Equal distances on a log axis represent ratios, not equal raw differences.'],['Question a bar chart','Two heights are 98 and 100.|A bar axis starting at 97 makes visible heights 1 and 3.|That can suggest a threefold difference instead of a small relative change.','For magnitude-comparison bars, a zero baseline usually preserves the intended visual ratio.']],
'Transforming or rescaling a picture does not create additional evidence.','Use scales that answer the question honestly and explain their meaning.');
group='04 · Probability foundations';
add('events','Outcomes, events and probability rules',
'Probability describes uncertainty within a specified model. An outcome is one possible result. An event is a collection of results you care about. Before calculating, define which outcomes are possible and whether they are equally likely.',
'Sample space = all possible outcomes.|Complement = outcomes outside an event.|Union = either event occurs, including overlap.',
'P(not A)=1−P(A); P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A and B)',
'The favorable-count divided by total-count rule applies when elementary outcomes are equally likely. Otherwise add their probability weights. Probabilities of disjoint exhaustive events sum to one. An event of probability zero in a continuous model requires different intuition from an impossible outcome in a finite sample space.',
[['A fair die','Outcomes are 1 through 6 with equal chance.|Even outcomes are 2,4,6.|Probability=3/6=1/2.','The counting rule is justified by fairness.'],['Use a complement','Chance of delay is 0.15.|No-delay chance=1−0.15.|This gives 0.85.','The two complementary events cover all outcomes.'],['Avoid double-counting','P(A)=0.4, P(B)=0.3 and overlap=0.1.|Add then subtract overlap: 0.4+0.3−0.1.|The union is 0.6.','Adding 0.4 and 0.3 alone would count shared probability twice.']],
'Equal-looking labels do not guarantee equally likely outcomes.','Define the probability model before counting events.');
add('conditional','Conditional probability and independence',
'Learning that one event happened can change the possibilities you consider. Conditional probability measures chance within that new information. Independence means that learning one event does not change the probability of the other, when the conditional probabilities are defined.',
'P(A given B) = chance of A after B is known.|Independent = joint probability factors into the product.|Disjoint = events cannot occur together.',
'P(A|B)=P(A and B)/P(B), for P(B)>0',
'Disjoint positive-probability events are not independent: observing one rules out the other. Independence may be plausible by design, but should not be assumed just because rows are stored separately. Conditional independence after knowing a third variable is different from unconditional independence.',
[['Condition a die result','A fair die is known to be even.|Remaining outcomes are 2,4,6; two exceed 3.|P(above 3 given even)=2/3.','The denominator is the remaining group, not all six outcomes.'],['Multiply independent events','Two independent fair tosses each have head chance 1/2.|Both heads probability=(1/2)×(1/2).|Result=1/4.','Independence justifies using unchanged probabilities.'],['Draw without replacement','A bag has 2 red and 1 blue ball.|After one red is drawn, red chance becomes 1/2.|Two reds probability=(2/3)×(1/2)=1/3.','The second probability changes because the draws are dependent.']],
'P(A|B) generally differs from P(B|A).','Conditioning changes what is known; independence states when that change does not matter.');
add('bayes','Bayes’ rule: update with evidence',
'An alert is evidence, not certainty. To interpret it, count all ways the alert could arise. Bayes’ rule combines the frequency of a situation before the evidence with how likely that situation is to produce the evidence.',
'Prior = probability before the new evidence.|Likelihood = probability of evidence under a proposed situation.|Posterior = probability after updating.',
'P(H|E)=P(E|H)P(H)/P(E)',
'The denominator normalizes weighted likelihoods across mutually exclusive exhaustive hypotheses. Base rates matter: a rare event can remain uncommon among alerts when false alerts arise from a much larger group. In continuous models, densities and integrals replace simple sums. A posterior conclusion depends on the assumptions and prior used.',
[['Interpret flagged items','Among 100 items, 8 faulty and 18 good items are flagged.|Total flags=26.|Faulty share=8/26≈30.77%.','The chance of a fault among flags is not the detection rate among faulty items.'],['Update two equally likely sources','Source A marks 60% of items; B marks 20%; prior shares are equal.|Weighted masses are 0.5×0.6=0.3 and 0.5×0.2=0.1.|Given a mark, A share=0.3/0.4=0.75.','The evidence raises A’s probability from 50% to 75%.'],['Update odds','Prior odds are 1:4; evidence likelihood ratio is 6.|Posterior odds ratio=(1/4)×6=1.5.|Probability=1.5/(1+1.5)=0.6.','Multiply odds, then normalize to obtain probability.']],
'Ignoring false alarms or the prior frequency can reverse an interpretation.','Posterior belief is prior belief reweighted by evidence.');
add('expectation','Random variables and expected values',
'A random variable gives a number to an uncertain result. Its expected value is a probability-weighted average. It describes a long-run or model-based center, not what must happen on the next observation. The expected value need not itself be a possible outcome.',
'Random variable = a numerical description of an uncertain outcome.|Expected value = sum or integral of values weighted by probability.|Variance = expected squared distance from the expected value.',
'E[X]=ΣxP(X=x); Var(X)=E[X²]−E[X]²',
'Expectation is linear even when variables are dependent. Variance of a sum includes covariance unless that covariance is zero. Finite expectations and variances are assumptions, not properties of every distribution. Expected cost is useful for decisions only when its probability and value model matches the actual task.',
[['A fair die average','Values 1 through 6 each have probability 1/6.|Sum=21; divide by 6.|Expectation=3.5.','No die face shows 3.5; expectation is not a guaranteed outcome.'],['A weighted score','Score 0 occurs with probability 0.8 and 10 with probability 0.2.|Expected score=0×0.8+10×0.2.|Result=2.','Rare larger outcomes contribute to the average.'],['Compute variance','X is 0 or 2, each with probability 1/2.|E[X]=1 and E[X²]=2.|Variance=2−1²=1.','Standard deviation is √1=1.']],
'Averages can hide large uncertainty or asymmetric consequences.','Expectation weights each possible value by how likely it is.');
add('counting','Counting arrangements and combinations',
'Counting gives probabilities only after you decide what counts as a distinct result. Choosing two people for a team is different from choosing a leader and an assistant. The first ignores order; the second gives positions different meanings.',
'Factorial n! = product from 1 through n, with 0!=1.|Permutation = an ordered selection.|Combination = an unordered selection.',
'C(n,k)=n!/[k!(n−k)!]; ordered selections=n!/(n−k)!',
'The multiplication principle counts successive choices with the appropriate number of options at each stage. Sampling with replacement differs from sampling without replacement. A combination factor in a binomial probability counts every arrangement producing the same success count. Do not mix a count of unordered events with probabilities for ordered sequences.',
[['Choose a pair','Choose 2 from A,B,C.|The pairs are AB,AC and BC.|There are 3 combinations.','AB and BA are the same unordered team.'],['Assign two roles','Choose leader then assistant from 3 people.|There are 3 leader choices and 2 remaining assistants.|Total=3×2=6.','Order matters because the roles differ.'],['Three independent fair tosses','Exactly two heads can occur as HHT, HTH or THH.|Each sequence has probability 1/8.|Total probability=3/8.','The factor 3 counts the different successful arrangements.']],
'State replacement and order conventions before applying a counting formula.','A probability calculation starts with a clear definition of distinct outcomes.');
group='05 · Probability distributions';
add('bernoulli','Bernoulli and categorical outcomes',
'One yes/no outcome can be coded as 1 or 0. A Bernoulli distribution assigns probability p to 1 and 1−p to 0. A categorical distribution extends this to several possible labels. The labels are mutually exclusive for one observation.',
'Bernoulli = one binary trial.|p = success probability, where success is merely the coded event.|Categorical = one outcome from several labels.',
'E[X]=p; Var(X)=p(1−p) for Bernoulli X',
'Calling an outcome success does not make it desirable; it just names the event coded 1. Category probabilities must be nonnegative and sum to one. Binary coding enables averages to represent proportions. Independent trials and a constant p are extra assumptions needed for the ordinary binomial count model.',
[['Calculate two moments','A flag has chance p=0.2.|Expected binary value=0.2.|Variance=0.2×0.8=0.16.','The average flag indicator estimates a probability.'],['Check a categorical model','Probabilities for red, blue, green are 0.5,0.3,0.2.|They are nonnegative.|Their sum is 1.','This is a valid categorical probability vector.'],['Average indicators','Observed codes are [1,0,1,1].|Sum=3 successes among 4.|Mean=3/4=0.75.','The indicator mean is the observed proportion.']],
'Do not assume different trials are independent merely because each has a Bernoulli marginal distribution.','A binary average is a proportion when the coding is 0 and 1.');
add('binomial','Binomial counts: successes in fixed trials',
'A binomial variable counts successes in a fixed number of independent trials that share the same success probability. It is useful for repeated yes/no outcomes when those assumptions are plausible. The count can range from zero to the number of trials.',
'n = fixed trial count.|p = common success probability.|k = requested success count.',
'P(K=k)=C(n,k)pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ; E[K]=np; Var(K)=np(1−p)',
'The combination term counts arrangements of successes. Independence and constant probability matter: repeated outcomes from one person may be correlated, while sampling without replacement from a small population changes probabilities. For the latter design, a hypergeometric model may be more appropriate. Approximation methods need adequate expected counts.',
[['Exactly one head','Two fair independent tosses permit HT or TH.|Each has probability 1/4.|Total=1/2.','The binomial count combines both arrangements.'],['No successes','Three independent trials have p=0.2.|Failure probability is 0.8 each.|P(K=0)=0.8³=0.512.','No success is still fairly likely with only three trials.'],['Mean and variance','For n=10,p=0.3, mean=np=3.|Variance=10×0.3×0.7=2.1.|Standard deviation=√2.1≈1.449.','An expected count of 3 does not force the observed count to be 3.']],
'A count of binary outcomes is not automatically binomial when trials depend on one another.','Check fixed trials, independence and a common probability before using the binomial formula.');
add('poisson','Poisson counts and exposure',
'The Poisson distribution models a nonnegative event count with mean λ. A constant-rate Poisson process additionally assumes appropriate independent increments. Exposure matters: doubling observation time doubles the expected count under a constant rate, not the rate itself.',
'λ = expected count for the specified exposure.|Rate = expected events per unit exposure.|Overdispersion = variability greater than a chosen model predicts.',
'P(K=k)=exp(−λ)λᵏ/k!; E[K]=Var(K)=λ',
'A Poisson count has equal mean and variance. Clustering, changing rates or unmeasured heterogeneity can violate this relation. Negative-binomial models can accommodate certain overdispersed counts. A count model should state its exposure; in regression, a log-exposure offset often ensures comparisons are rates rather than raw counts.',
[['Zero events','Expected count λ=2 per hour.|P(K=0)=exp(−2).|This is approximately 0.1353.','Zero events has about 13.53% probability under this model.'],['Change exposure','Rate is 3 events/hour.|Observe for 2 hours.|Expected count=3×2=6.','The rate remains 3 per hour; λ for this interval becomes 6.'],['Check model spread','Repeated comparable intervals have mean count 4 and variance 20.|Poisson would predict variance 4.|The large discrepancy calls for investigation.','Overdispersion can signal clustering or model mismatch.']],
'Do not treat unequal observation periods as equally exposed counts.','A count is meaningful only with its exposure and variability assumptions.');
add('normal','Normal distributions and standardized values',
'A normal distribution is a symmetric bell-shaped model described by a mean and standard deviation. Many methods use normal approximations for estimators even when individual observations are not normal. These are different claims and should not be confused.',
'μ = mean.|σ = positive standard deviation.|Standard normal = normal distribution with mean 0 and standard deviation 1.',
'z=(x−μ)/σ; approximately 68%,95%,99.7% within 1,2,3 σ for a normal model',
'The familiar coverage percentages apply to a normal distribution, not arbitrary data. A normal density is continuous, so an exact single point has zero probability; intervals carry probability. Since the normal model extends across all real numbers, it can be unsuitable for strongly bounded or skewed measurements. Check the purpose and approximation quality.',
[['Standardize a value','Mean=100, standard deviation=15, value=130.|Difference=30.|z=30/15=2.','130 lies two standard deviations above the mean.'],['Use a model interval','Under N(50,5²), two standard deviations equal 10.|The interval is [40,60].|Normal coverage is about 95.45%.','This is a distribution interval for values, not a confidence interval for an estimated mean.'],['Recover original units','A standardized value is −1.5 with mean 20 and σ=4.|Original value=20+(−1.5)×4.|Result=14.','Negative z means below the mean, not necessarily a negative measurement.']],
'A z-score transformation does not make non-normal data normal.','Separate the distribution of observations from the distribution of an estimator.');
add('continuous','Uniform, exponential and other distribution families',
'A continuous density gives probability through area over a range. Uniform distributions assign constant density within an interval. Exponential distributions model waiting time in a constant-rate Poisson process. Other families describe bounded probabilities, heavy tails or uncertain variance.',
'Density = probability per unit of the variable.|Exponential rate λ = inverse mean waiting time.|Student t = a symmetric heavier-tailed family used in several inferential procedures.',
'Uniform[a,b]: density=1/(b−a); exponential P(T>t)=exp(−λt)',
'An exponential model has the memoryless property, which many real waiting-time processes do not satisfy. Beta distributions describe values between zero and one; gamma and Weibull families can model positive durations; chi-square and F distributions arise in variance-related inference. Choose a family for its assumptions and support, not just its name.',
[['Uniform interval','A point is uniform on [0,10].|The interval [2,5] has width 3.|Probability=3/10=0.3.','Probability depends on relative interval length.'],['Exponential waiting','Rate=2 per hour; wait threshold=0.5 hour.|P(T>0.5)=exp(−2×0.5).|Result≈0.3679.','About 36.79% of waits exceed half an hour under this model.'],['Density above one','Uniform[0,0.25] has density 4.|Area over its full support is 4×0.25.|Total probability=1.','A density height may exceed 1; a probability may not.']],
'A density value at a point is not that point’s probability.','Continuous probability is area, and each distribution family makes specific assumptions.');
group='06 · Sampling and resampling';
add('sampling','Random, stratified and cluster sampling',
'Sampling design determines how observations enter a study. A simple random sample gives every subset of a given size equal selection chance. Stratified sampling samples within predefined groups. Cluster sampling selects groups, such as classrooms, and observes units within them.',
'Stratum = a predefined population subgroup.|Cluster = a group sampled as a unit or stage.|Sampling weight = often inverse inclusion probability.',
'Inverse-probability weight = 1 / inclusion probability',
'Stratification can improve coverage and precision for important subgroups. Cluster samples can be cheaper but often contain correlated observations, reducing effective information relative to the same number of independently sampled units. A systematic sample uses a random start and regular interval, but periodic structure can cause problems. Analysis should respect the actual design.',
[['Stratify by group','A school has 60 juniors and 40 seniors.|Sample 10% within each group.|Take 6 juniors and 4 seniors.','Both strata are represented proportionally.'],['Use selection weights','One unit has inclusion chance 0.1 and another 0.2.|Inverse weights are 10 and 5.|The less likely selected unit represents more population units.','Unequal selection often needs weights for population summaries.'],['Recognize clustering','You sample 5 classrooms with 20 pupils each.|There are 100 pupils but only 5 sampled classrooms.|Shared classroom influences can create dependence.','Ordinary independent-row standard errors may be too small.']],
'Random sampling and random treatment assignment serve different purposes.','Choose a design for the target population and analyze according to that design.');
add('bias','Bias, nonresponse and representativeness',
'Bias is systematic error from a procedure. Random variation changes across samples; bias can persist even in a huge dataset. Selection, nonresponse, measurement and survivorship can all distort conclusions. Ask who is missing and how answers were measured.',
'Selection bias = the observed group differs systematically from the target.|Nonresponse = selected units fail to provide data.|Survivorship bias = only units remaining visible are studied.',
'Total error can include systematic bias and random variability',
'Increasing sample size helps with random error under a stable design but does not necessarily fix bias. Weighting can adjust for observed selection factors, but not guarantee correction for unmeasured differences. Leading questions and changing measurement definitions can also introduce systematic errors. Compare the sampling frame with the actual target population.',
[['Convenience sampling','Ask only visitors to a sports club about weekly exercise.|They may exercise more than non-visitors.|A larger club sample still excludes many other people.','More observations do not repair this coverage problem automatically.'],['Survivorship','Study only shops still open after five years.|Closed shops are absent.|A reported average growth rate may overstate the experience of all starting shops.','Define whether the target includes failures.'],['Measurement bias','A scale reads 2 kilograms too high each time.|Averaging 1,000 readings reduces some noise.|The 2-kilogram systematic offset remains.','Repeated measurement does not remove a fixed calibration error.']],
'Do not equate a large dataset with an unbiased one.','Representativeness is about the selection and measurement process, not just sample size.');
add('clt','Sampling distributions, standard error and the CLT',
'An estimator would change if you collected another sample. Its sampling distribution describes those possible values. Standard error measures their spread. The central limit theorem explains why many properly normalized sample means approach a normal distribution under suitable assumptions.',
'Sampling distribution = distribution of an estimator across repeated samples.|Standard error = standard deviation of that distribution.|CLT = a limit theorem for standardized sums or averages.',
'SE(mean)=σ/√n for independent identically distributed values with finite variance',
'The CLT concerns averages, not a transformation of the original data into a normal distribution. No universal sample-size cutoff guarantees an adequate approximation; skewness, tails and dependence matter. Standard deviation describes individual variation, while standard error describes uncertainty in an estimate. Correlation can make nominal n overstate effective sample size.',
[['Compute standard error','Population standard deviation is 12 and n=36.|√36=6.|SE=12/6=2.','Individual observations can vary by 12 while the sample mean varies by about 2.'],['Increase sample size','Raise n from 36 to 144.|The square root doubles from 6 to 12.|SE falls from 2 to 1.','Four times as many independent observations halves SE.'],['Interpret repeated means','Repeated samples give different averages.|Their histogram can approach a bell shape.|The individual observations may remain strongly skewed.','The theorem concerns the estimator’s distribution.']],
'Do not report standard error as if it described the spread of individual observations.','Data variability and estimate uncertainty are different quantities.');
add('bootstrap','Bootstrap intervals and permutation tests',
'Resampling reuses observed data to approximate a reference distribution. Bootstrapping usually samples observations with replacement to study estimator variability. Permutation methods rearrange labels under a justified null or randomization scheme to test a hypothesis. They answer different questions.',
'Bootstrap sample = a same-size resample with replacement in the ordinary iid method.|Permutation = an allowed rearrangement under the test’s null/design.|Percentile interval = selected quantiles of bootstrap estimates.',
'Bootstrap: resample → compute statistic → repeat',
'A bootstrap approximates uncertainty using the empirical distribution; it does not create genuinely independent new evidence. Cluster or time-series data often need cluster or block resampling. Permuting labels requires exchangeability under the null or a valid randomization design. Simple percentile intervals can have poor coverage in difficult small-sample or biased-estimator settings.',
[['One bootstrap replicate','Original sample is [2,4,6].|A valid resample is [2,2,6].|Its mean is 10/3≈3.3333.','Repetition is allowed because sampling uses replacement.'],['Read a bootstrap interval','Suppose 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of bootstrap means are 9 and 14.|Use those as percentile endpoints.|Report [9,14] with the procedure and assumptions.','This is an approximate method, not guaranteed coverage for every problem.'],['A permutation idea','Four outcomes are assigned to two groups of size two.|Shuffle group labels only as allowed by the null design.|Recompute the mean difference for each allowed shuffle.','The rearranged differences form a null reference distribution.']],
'Resample at the unit where independence or randomization is justified.','Bootstrap explores estimator uncertainty; permutation explores a null comparison.');
add('size','Planning sample size and precision',
'Planning starts with the question and the uncertainty or effect size that matters. The required sample size depends on variation, desired precision, confidence level and design. There is no single sample size that is adequate for every study.',
'Margin of error = critical multiplier times standard error.|Precision = how narrow an uncertainty interval is.|Design effect = variance relative to a specified simple sampling design.',
'Approximate n for a mean margin E: n=(zσ/E)², rounded up',
'Planning formulas rely on assumed variability and sampling conditions. Add allowances for nonresponse and clustering when justified. Estimating a proportion often uses p(1−p), with p=0.5 giving the largest binomial variance. Planning for hypothesis-test power needs an effect, error rate and analysis model rather than only an interval width.',
[['Plan a mean estimate','Use z=1.96, anticipated σ=10 and desired margin E=2.|n=(1.96×10/2)²=96.04.|Round up to 97.','This is an approximate independent-sample calculation.'],['Allow for response rate','You need 100 completed responses.|Anticipated response rate is 80%.|Invite at least 100/0.8=125 under that planning assumption.','Invitations and completed observations are not the same.'],['Tighten precision','You want half the original margin with other factors fixed.|Sample size is proportional to 1/E².|Required n multiplies by 4.','Halving a margin can be costly.']],
'Do not treat guessed planning inputs as guaranteed future outcomes.','Specify what precision or effect you need before calculating a sample size.');
group='07 · Estimating unknown quantities';
add('estimation','Point estimates, bias and consistency',
'A point estimate is one number used to summarize an unknown population quantity. Its quality depends on its behavior across possible samples, not just one successful result. Bias is systematic offset; variance is sampling variability. An estimator can trade one for the other.',
'Estimator = a rule applied to data.|Bias = expected estimate minus the target parameter.|Consistency = approaching the target as sample size grows under stated conditions.',
'Mean squared estimation error = variance + bias²',
'Unbiasedness does not mean every sample estimate is correct. A biased estimator may have lower mean squared error than an unbiased but unstable estimator. Consistency is an asymptotic property and says little by itself about a small sample. The population, sampling process and target parameter must be specified before these properties have meaning.',
[['Estimate a mean','Observe [4,6,8].|Add to get 18.|Divide by 3 to estimate the mean as 6.','Another sample would usually give another estimate.'],['Combine bias and variance','An estimator has bias 2 and variance 3.|Square the bias: 4.|MSE=4+3=7.','Systematic error and random variability both contribute.'],['Compare estimators','A has bias 0, variance 9.|B has bias 1, variance 3.|MSE values are 9 and 4.','B has lower MSE in this hypothetical comparison despite its bias.']],
'Do not call an estimator unbiased simply because one estimate looks reasonable.','Evaluate an estimation rule relative to its target and sampling behavior.');
add('likelihood','Likelihood, maximum likelihood and standard models',
'Likelihood compares parameter choices by how compatible they make the observed data under a model. Maximum likelihood selects the parameter giving the largest likelihood. It does not assign probabilities to parameters unless a Bayesian model with a prior is added.',
'Likelihood = data probability or density viewed as a function of parameters.|MLE = maximum likelihood estimate.|Log-likelihood = logarithm of likelihood, often easier to calculate.',
'For independent Bernoulli data, p̂ = successes / trials',
'Taking logs converts products into sums without changing which positive likelihood is largest. The model and independence assumptions remain essential. Density values are not probabilities of exact continuous observations. Numerical optimization may be needed, and some models have boundary or nonexistent finite maxima.',
[['Estimate coin probability','Observe 7 successes in 10 independent trials.|Observed fraction=7/10.|Bernoulli MLE is 0.7.','This estimates probability; it does not guarantee seven successes next time.'],['Compare parameters','A specified HHT sequence has likelihood 0.5³=0.125 at p=0.5.|At p=0.8 it is 0.8²×0.2=0.128.|The second is slightly larger.','This only compares those two proposed parameter values.'],['Avoid tiny products','Two event probabilities are 0.1 and 0.01.|Joint probability=0.001 under independence.|Log joint=ln(0.1)+ln(0.01)=ln(0.001).','Logarithms simplify long products and improve numerical handling.']],
'The likelihood need not sum to one over parameter choices.','MLE chooses parameters that best support the observed data within the chosen model.');
add('confidence','Confidence intervals: a range with a procedure',
'A confidence interval is produced by a rule whose repeated-sampling coverage is specified. A 95% procedure covers the fixed population parameter in about 95% of repeated valid uses under its assumptions. It is not a claim that 95% of individual observations lie inside this interval.',
'Estimate = center of the interval.|Standard error = uncertainty scale for the estimate.|Critical value = multiplier determined by the reference distribution and confidence level.',
'Interval = estimate ± critical value × standard error',
'For a normal population with unknown variance, a one-sample mean interval uses a t critical value with n−1 degrees of freedom. Large-sample normal approximations may be reasonable in other settings but need justification. A frequentist confidence interval and a Bayesian credible interval have different interpretations. Wider coverage levels ordinarily require wider intervals.',
[['Normal approximation','Estimate=50, SE=2, critical value=1.96.|Margin=3.92.|Endpoints are 46.08 and 53.92.','Approximate 95% interval: [46.08,53.92].'],['A supplied t multiplier','Mean=10, SE=1 and appropriate t critical value=2.262.|Margin=2.262.|Interval=[7.738,12.262].','Using 1.96 automatically would give a narrower interval.'],['Interpret coverage','Imagine 100 repeated studies using a valid 95% procedure.|About 95 intervals would cover the parameter on average.|Any particular realized interval either covers it or does not.','The rate belongs to the procedure under its assumptions.']],
'Do not interpret a confidence interval as the range of 95% of the data.','An uncertainty interval needs its target, method and assumptions.');
add('proportion-interval','Estimating a proportion and its uncertainty',
'A sample proportion is the fraction of observations in a category. Its uncertainty depends on sample size, probability and sampling design. Simple normal intervals can behave poorly near zero or one or with small samples; score-based intervals such as Wilson are often more reliable.',
'p̂ = observed success fraction.|Wilson interval = interval formed by inverting a score test.|Wald interval = the simple estimate-plus-or-minus normal standard-error interval.',
'Approximate SE(p̂)=√[p̂(1−p̂)/n] for independent Bernoulli sampling',
'The usual Wald interval can extend outside [0,1] or collapse when no successes are observed. Wilson and exact binomial approaches address some of these problems in different ways; exact intervals can be conservative. None fixes a biased sample. Finite-population corrections or survey-design methods may be needed outside iid sampling.',
[['Calculate an approximate SE','60 successes among 100 give p̂=0.6.|SE=√(0.6×0.4/100).|SE≈0.0490.','A rough 1.96-SE margin is about 0.096.'],['Recognize zero-count trouble','No successes occur in 10 trials.|The Wald formula gives SE=0.|That cannot establish that the population probability is exactly zero.','Use an appropriate binomial interval instead.'],['A Wilson zero-count example','For 0 of 10 and z=1.96, the Wilson lower endpoint is 0.|Upper endpoint is z²/(n+z²)=3.8416/13.8416.|Upper≈0.2775.','The approximate 95% Wilson interval retains substantial uncertainty.']],
'Zero observed events do not imply zero event probability.','Choose a proportion interval suited to the sample size and event counts.');
add('bayesian','Bayesian estimation and credible intervals',
'Bayesian estimation combines a prior distribution with a likelihood to obtain a posterior distribution. The posterior expresses parameter uncertainty within that model. A credible interval contains a specified amount of posterior probability; it differs conceptually from repeated-sampling confidence coverage.',
'Prior = distribution before these data.|Posterior = distribution after updating.|Posterior predictive = predictions averaged over posterior parameter uncertainty.',
'Beta(a,b) prior + s successes and f failures → Beta(a+s,b+f)',
'For a beta posterior, the mean is a/(a+b) using the updated parameters. The posterior mode is different and needs boundary cases handled carefully. Prior sensitivity matters especially with little data. A credible interval is a probability statement conditioned on the likelihood and prior, not a guarantee that the model assumptions are correct.',
[['Update a uniform prior','Start with Beta(1,1).|Observe 3 successes and 1 failure.|Posterior=Beta(4,2), mean=4/6≈0.6667.','The posterior mean differs from the sample proportion 0.75.'],['Compare prior strength','Beta(10,10) with the same data becomes Beta(13,11).|Posterior mean=13/24≈0.5417.|The stronger symmetric prior has more influence.','Prior choices should be explained and checked.'],['Interpret a credible interval','A fitted posterior places 95% probability between 0.2 and 0.5.|That is a 95% credible interval under this model.|It is not automatically a 95%-coverage frequentist procedure.','The interpretation is conditional on the modeling choices.']],
'A prior is not an extra batch of observed data, even when count analogies are useful.','Bayesian conclusions combine evidence with explicit prior assumptions.');
group='08 · Testing claims';
add('pvalues','Null hypotheses, p-values and error types',
'A hypothesis test compares observed evidence with what a specified null model predicts. A p-value is the probability, under that null and test procedure, of a statistic at least as extreme as observed. It is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true.',
'Null hypothesis = the reference claim being tested.|Type I error = rejecting a true null.|Type II error = failing to reject a false null for a specified alternative.',
'Reject when p ≤ preselected α under the chosen testing procedure',
'Choose the hypothesis, sidedness, statistic and analysis plan before examining results. A significance threshold controls an error property under assumptions; it does not create a sharp boundary between truth and falsehood. Report estimated effects, uncertainty and design limitations. A large p-value can reflect limited information rather than equivalence.',
[['Use a planned threshold','α=0.05 was chosen before analysis.|A valid test gives p=0.03.|Reject the null at this threshold.','This does not mean the null has a 3% probability of being true.'],['Failing to reject','A test gives p=0.4.|The evidence is not sufficiently unusual under this test’s null.|Do not claim the groups are proven equal.','A lack of evidence can result from imprecision.'],['Define error types','The null says a process has not changed.|Rejecting it when unchanged is Type I error.|Missing a real change is Type II error.','Different tasks can assign different consequences to these errors.']],
'Do not interpret p as the chance that a result happened “by chance” without specifying the null model.','A p-value measures incompatibility with a specified reference model, not truth probability.');
add('one-mean','One-sample tests for a mean',
'A one-sample mean test asks whether a population mean matches a proposed value. It compares the observed difference with the standard error. With unknown population variance, the usual t statistic uses the sample standard deviation and n−1 degrees of freedom.',
'μ₀ = proposed population mean.|t statistic = mean difference divided by estimated SE.|Degrees of freedom = n−1 in the standard one-sample t test.',
't=(x̄−μ₀)/(s/√n)',
'Exact small-sample t inference assumes independent normal observations. Robust large-sample use may be reasonable under suitable conditions, but strong outliers or dependence can undermine it. One-sided tests address a preselected direction; choosing a direction after seeing the result invalidates the nominal procedure.',
[['Compute the statistic','n=25, mean=12, sample SD=5, null mean=10.|SE=5/√25=1.|t=(12−10)/1=2 with 24 degrees of freedom.','The statistic still needs its t reference distribution for a p-value.'],['A smaller difference','Keep SE=1 but use mean=10.5.|Difference from 10 is 0.5.|t=0.5.','The observed difference is half a standard error.'],['A dependence warning','Measure the same object 25 times.|Those measurements may share object-specific effects.|They are not necessarily 25 independent objects.','The appropriate unit determines the standard error.']],
'Do not substitute the observation SD for the SE of the mean.','A mean test compares the observed gap with uncertainty in that gap.');
add('two-means','Comparing two independent means',
'Two independent groups may have different average outcomes. Welch’s t method compares their mean difference without assuming equal variances. A pooled equal-variance test makes an additional assumption. Use a method matching the actual grouping and dependence.',
'Independent groups = observations in one group are independent of the other under the model.|Welch test = mean comparison allowing different variances.|Mean difference = group A mean minus group B mean.',
'SE(difference)=√(sA²/nA+sB²/nB)',
'Welch’s reference degrees of freedom are estimated using both variance components. The effect estimate and its interval are usually more informative than a reject/not-reject statement alone. Random assignment supports causal interpretation when design conditions hold; separate observational groups may differ for other reasons.',
[['Calculate an SE','A: mean 10, SD 4, n=16. B: mean 8, SD 3, n=9.|SE=√(16/16+9/9)=√2.|Difference=2; statistic≈1.4142.','A p-value requires the appropriate Welch degrees of freedom.'],['Compare uncertainty','If both sample sizes quadruple with similar SDs, each variance contribution divides by 4.|The SE is halved.|The same mean difference becomes more precisely estimated.','Effect size does not grow simply because n grows.'],['Identify nonindependent groups','Before and after values come from the same people.|Each before value is linked to an after value.|Analyze paired differences instead of treating groups as unrelated.','Design determines the comparison method.']],
'Do not assume equal variances merely because the groups have equal sample sizes.','Compare means using the uncertainty structure of the actual study.');
add('paired','Paired data and repeated measurements',
'When each observation has a natural partner, the within-pair difference is often the quantity of interest. Examples include before-and-after measurements on the same person or matched items. A paired mean test becomes a one-sample test of the differences.',
'Pair = linked observations belonging together.|Difference = after minus before under the stated convention.|Repeated measures = multiple observations on the same unit.',
't = mean(differences) / [SD(differences)/√number of pairs]',
'Pairing can remove stable between-person variation, improving precision. The pairs themselves need appropriate independence, and exact small-sample t inference concerns the distribution of differences. More than two repeated observations may require repeated-measures or mixed models rather than many separate paired tests.',
[['Calculate changes','Before=[5,7,9], after=[6,9,10].|Pairwise differences=[1,2,1].|Mean difference=4/3≈1.3333.','Pairing keeps each person’s change attached to that person.'],['Compute a paired statistic','Differences are [1,2,3,4].|Mean=2.5; sample variance=5/3; SE=√(5/3)/2≈0.6455.|t≈3.873 with 3 degrees of freedom.','The SD used is the SD of differences.'],['Why pairing matters','People start at very different levels.|Each rises by the same amount.|Within-person differences can be stable despite large cross-person spread.','Analyzing unrelated group means can discard useful pairing information.']],
'Do not pair observations simply by sorting each group. Pairing must come from the design.','For paired questions, begin by calculating valid within-pair differences.');
add('proportion-tests','Testing one or two proportions',
'Proportion tests compare event rates with a benchmark or between groups. Counts, denominators and independence are central. Large-sample z tests need adequate expected event and nonevent counts; small or sparse data may call for exact or alternative procedures.',
'Null proportion p₀ = benchmark under the null.|Pooled proportion = combined successes divided by combined trials.|Two-proportion null = often pA=pB.',
'One-proportion z=(p̂−p₀)/√[p₀(1−p₀)/n]',
'For an equality test of two proportions, a common large-sample null SE uses the pooled rate. A confidence interval for the difference typically uses a different construction, so test and interval SE formulas should not be mixed automatically. Paired binary outcomes require methods such as McNemar’s test rather than an independent two-proportion test.',
[['One rate versus a benchmark','60 of 100 succeed; null p₀=0.5.|Null SE=√(0.5×0.5/100)=0.05.|z=(0.6−0.5)/0.05=2.','The null probability is used in this test’s SE.'],['Pool two groups','A has 30/50 successes and B has 20/50.|Combined successes=50 out of 100.|Pooled rate=0.5.','The pooled rate estimates the common probability under equality.'],['Sparse counts','Only 1 event occurs among 8 trials.|A normal approximation may be poor.|Use an appropriate binomial or small-sample method.','A spreadsheet formula is not a substitute for checking its assumptions.']],
'Counts from repeated or clustered units are not independent Bernoulli trials by default.','State the event, denominator and design before comparing proportions.');
group='09 · Comparing groups responsibly';
add('chi-square','Chi-square tables and categorical association',
'A contingency table cross-classifies two categorical variables. A chi-square independence test compares observed cell counts with counts expected if the variables were independent. It measures departures across the table, not a direction or practical size by itself.',
'Observed count O = actual cell count.|Expected count E = count predicted under the null.|Degrees of freedom = (rows−1)(columns−1) for the usual independence table.',
'E = row total × column total / grand total; χ² = Σ(O−E)²/E',
'The test needs suitable independent sampling and adequate expected counts for its asymptotic approximation. Sparse tables may call for exact or simulation-based methods. Fisher’s exact test is a common option for small 2×2 tables. A significant association does not establish causation. Report proportions and an effect measure with the table.',
[['Calculate one expected count','A row total is 40, column total is 30 and grand total is 100.|Expected count=40×30/100.|E=12.','Expected counts come from marginal totals, not arbitrary equal allocation.'],['One contribution','Observed=18 and expected=12.|Squared difference=36.|Contribution=36/12=3.','Add analogous contributions from every cell.'],['Degrees of freedom','A table has 3 rows and 2 columns.|Compute (3−1)(2−1).|df=2.','Use the reference distribution appropriate to the full table.']],
'Small expected counts can make the ordinary chi-square approximation unreliable.','Compare observed category combinations with a clearly defined independence model.');
add('anova','ANOVA: comparing several means',
'Analysis of variance, or ANOVA, tests whether all group means match in a specified model. It compares between-group variation with within-group variation. The usual one-way test does not tell you which groups differ when its null is rejected.',
'Between-group variation = separation of group means.|Within-group variation = spread of observations around their own group means.|F statistic = ratio of mean squares.',
'F = MS_between / MS_within',
'Classical one-way ANOVA assumes independent observations, approximately normal within-group errors for exact inference and equal error variances. Welch ANOVA relaxes equal variances. Planned contrasts and adjusted follow-up comparisons address specific group differences. Repeated-measures data need a corresponding model rather than ordinary independent-group ANOVA.',
[['Read supplied mean squares','Between-group mean square=12.|Within-group mean square=3.|F=12/3=4.','A p-value also needs the numerator and denominator degrees of freedom.'],['Interpret rejection','Three groups have means 10,10 and 16.|An ANOVA can detect a difference among means.|Rejection does not say all three pairs differ.','Follow-up questions need specific contrasts and multiplicity control.'],['Check the design','Each person tries three methods.|Their three outcomes are linked.|An independent-group ANOVA ignores that dependence.','Use a repeated-measures or suitable mixed-model analysis.']],
'Do not perform many unadjusted pairwise tests as if the overall error rate were unchanged.','ANOVA asks an overall mean-comparison question; follow-up claims need their own justification.');
add('rank-tests','Rank tests and nonparametric comparisons',
'Rank methods use relative order rather than raw numerical distances. They can help when a chosen parametric model is unsuitable, but they still have assumptions. “Nonparametric” does not mean assumption-free or automatically more powerful.',
'Rank = position in an ordered list.|Mann–Whitney = a two-independent-group rank-based comparison.|Wilcoxon signed-rank = a paired-difference procedure with symmetry assumptions for common interpretations.',
'Replace values by pooled ranks; construct the test statistic specified by the method',
'Mann–Whitney generally concerns distributions or pairwise ordering probabilities; interpreting it purely as a median test needs additional equal-shape/location assumptions. Kruskal–Wallis extends rank comparison to several independent groups. Sign tests use only positive and negative differences, discarding size information. Ties and dependence require appropriate handling.',
[['Assign ranks','Pooled values are 3,8 and 5.|Sort to 3,5,8.|Their ranks are 1,2,3.','Ranks preserve order but not the original numerical gaps.'],['Handle ties','Values are 2,2,9.|The two 2s occupy positions 1 and 2.|Assign each average rank 1.5, and 9 rank 3.','The tied values receive equal rank.'],['A sign-test count','Paired changes are [2,−1,3,4,0].|Exclude the zero under the usual sign-test convention.|There are 3 positive and 1 negative nonzero changes.','The reference calculation uses the four nonzero signs under its null.']],
'A rank-test result does not automatically identify a difference in means or medians.','Choose a rank method for its target and assumptions, not just because data looks non-normal.');
add('multiple','Multiple testing and selective analysis',
'Testing many claims creates more opportunities for false positives. Multiplicity procedures control a stated error measure across a defined family of tests. Selectively reporting only the small p-values hides the search that produced them.',
'Family-wise error rate = chance of at least one false rejection in a family.|False discovery rate = expected false-discovery proportion under its definition.|Bonferroni = compare each p-value with α/m.',
'Bonferroni threshold = family α / number of tests',
'Bonferroni is simple and can be conservative. Holm provides a sequential family-wise procedure. Benjamini–Hochberg controls FDR under its required conditions and has variants for other dependence settings. Repeated interim looks and data-driven subgroup searches also create multiplicity. Define the family and analysis plan, and distinguish exploratory from confirmatory work.',
[['Adjust a threshold','Five planned tests share family α=0.05.|Bonferroni threshold=0.05/5.|Each p-value must be at most 0.01 for this rule.','An unadjusted 0.03 no longer meets this threshold.'],['At least one false positive','Under 20 independent true-null tests at 0.05 each, no false rejection chance is 0.95²⁰.|At least one chance is 1−0.95²⁰.|Approximately 0.6415.','Independence is assumed for this particular calculation.'],['Selective reporting','An analyst tries 30 subgroup definitions and reports only the smallest p-value.|The displayed test hides the search.|A nominal single-test interpretation is misleading.','Report the analysis process and account for selection.']],
'Correction after an undisclosed search does not automatically make the entire analysis confirmatory.','The set of questions you tried matters, not just the result you reported.');
add('power','Effect sizes, power and equivalence',
'An effect size describes how large a difference is. Power is the probability a procedure rejects a false null under a specified alternative. Equivalence asks whether differences are sufficiently small within a prechosen margin; it is not established by a nonsignificant ordinary test.',
'Effect size = magnitude on raw or standardized units.|Power = 1−Type II error probability at a specified alternative.|Equivalence margin = largest difference considered practically unimportant.',
'Standardized mean difference = mean difference / chosen reference SD',
'Power depends on true effect, noise, sample size, design and error threshold. Plan with scientifically meaningful effects rather than computing “observed power” from the result. Standardized effects need the denominator convention and context. Equivalence and noninferiority have explicit directional or interval hypotheses and require appropriate designs and tests.',
[['Standardize a difference','Mean difference=4 and reference SD=8.|Divide 4 by 8.|Standardized effect=0.5.','Whether this is useful depends on the actual outcome and context.'],['Interpret power','A planned test has 80% power for a specific effect.|Under that alternative, it misses the effect about 20% of the time.|Power changes if the true effect changes.','80% is not the probability that a significant finding is true.'],['Equivalence example','A prespecified acceptable difference is between −2 and 2.|A valid 90% interval for a 5%-level TOST analysis is [−1,1].|It lies entirely within the equivalence margins.','This supports equivalence under that procedure and assumptions.']],
'Failure to reject zero difference does not prove practical equivalence.','Report magnitude and uncertainty, and plan for the question that matters.');
group='10 · Statistical models';
add('regression','Simple linear regression and least squares',
'Linear regression predicts a numerical outcome from an input using a fitted line. The slope describes the predicted change per input unit, and the intercept is the prediction at input zero. Least squares chooses the line minimizing the sum of squared residuals.',
'Residual = observed value minus fitted value in this lab.|Slope = predicted output change for one input unit.|Intercept = fitted output at input zero.',
'ŷ=b₀+b₁x; residual=y−ŷ',
'A fitted slope is an association unless a justified design supports a causal interpretation. Extrapolating far beyond observed inputs can be misleading. Usual inferential formulas assume a model for errors and their dependence; a line can be fitted even when those assumptions fail. Always inspect residuals and influential observations.',
[['Predict a value','Fitted rule is ŷ=2+3x.|For x=4, compute 2+12.|Prediction=14.','The intercept and slope contribute different parts.'],['Calculate a residual','Observed y=16 and fitted value=14.|Residual=16−14=2.|Squared residual=4.','A positive residual means the observation is above the fitted line.'],['Fit two exact points','Points are (1,5) and (3,9).|Slope=(9−5)/(3−1)=2.|Intercept=5−2×1=3.','The line is ŷ=3+2x, but two points alone give weak validation.']],
'A fitted coefficient is not automatically a causal effect.','Regression links predictions to inputs and checks remaining errors.');
add('multiple-regression','Multiple regression, adjustment and collinearity',
'Multiple regression includes several predictors. A coefficient describes the model’s change in predicted outcome for one predictor while holding the others fixed. If predictors move almost together, separating their contributions can be difficult even when predictions remain useful.',
'Adjusted coefficient = a coefficient conditional on the included predictors.|Collinearity = predictors contain overlapping linear information.|Interaction = one predictor’s effect depends on another.',
'ŷ=b₀+b₁x₁+b₂x₂+…',
'Adjustment is only as good as the variables, functional form and causal assumptions chosen. Including a mediator or collider can change or bias a causal question; more predictors are not always better. Strong collinearity inflates uncertainty and can make coefficients unstable. Interpret interactions with the relevant main effects and input ranges.',
[['Use two inputs','Rule is ŷ=1+2x₁+3x₂.|At x₁=2,x₂=1, predict 1+4+3.|Result=8.','Changing x₁ alone by 1 changes this prediction by 2.'],['Identify redundancy','Predictors are height in meters and the same height in centimeters.|One is exactly 100 times the other.|Their separate unrestricted effects are not identifiable.','Remove or reparameterize redundant information.'],['Interpret an interaction','Rule contains 2x+3xz.|The slope in x is 2+3z.|At z=0 it is 2; at z=1 it is 5.','A single main-effect coefficient is not the whole slope when interactions are present.']],
'Statistical adjustment cannot automatically remove unmeasured confounding.','Interpret each coefficient in the context of the other variables and the model.');
add('logistic','Logistic regression and generalized linear models',
'Logistic regression models a binary outcome probability using a linear score passed through a sigmoid. It is one generalized linear model, or GLM. Other links and distributions handle counts and other outcomes. A predicted probability is distinct from a yes/no decision threshold.',
'Logit = log odds.|Odds = p/(1−p).|GLM = a model combining a mean link with a chosen response distribution.',
'p=1/[1+exp(−z)], where z=b₀+b₁x+…',
'A logistic coefficient changes log odds; exp(coefficient) is a conditional odds multiplier per input unit. Odds ratios can differ greatly from risk ratios when outcomes are common. Poisson regression often uses a log mean link and an exposure offset. Check calibration, dependence, separation and distributional assumptions rather than treating a model name as a guarantee.',
[['Middle score','At score z=0, exp(−z)=1.|Probability=1/(1+1).|p=0.5.','A zero score is not a zero probability.'],['Convert odds','Odds are 3:1.|Probability=3/(3+1).|p=0.75.','An odds value of 3 corresponds to 75%, not 300%.'],['Interpret a coefficient','A coefficient equals ln 2.|Increasing its predictor by 1 doubles modeled odds, holding other inputs fixed.|Probability change depends on the starting odds.','An odds multiplier is not a constant probability-point increase.']],
'Do not describe an odds ratio as a risk ratio without justification.','Match a model’s link and outcome family to the question and data.');
add('diagnostics','Residuals, assumptions and influential points',
'Model diagnostics examine what a fitted model fails to explain. Residual patterns can reveal curvature, unequal spread, dependence or outliers. A single point can have a large effect on a fitted line if it lies far from other predictor values.',
'Residual plot = errors against fitted values or inputs.|Heteroscedasticity = nonconstant error variance.|Leverage = unusual predictor position relative to the design.',
'Residual = observed − fitted; residual plots should be read in context',
'A residual pattern is a clue, not an automatic diagnosis. Robust standard errors can address some variance misspecification but do not fix an incorrect mean function or causal design. High leverage and large residuals contribute differently to influence. Check sensitivity with and without questionable points, investigate provenance and report principled decisions.',
[['A curved residual pattern','Residuals tend positive at low and high x, negative in the middle.|The line systematically misses a curve.|Consider a justified nonlinear term or other model.','Adding more data alone may not fix the wrong functional form.'],['A widening spread','Residual spread grows with fitted values.|Constant-variance standard errors may be inappropriate.|Consider suitable variance modeling or robust inference.','The fitted mean may still be useful even when spread assumptions fail.'],['A distant input','Most x values are 1–10; one is 100.|That observation has unusual leverage.|Check how strongly it changes the fit and whether it is valid.','Leverage is about input position, not just a large outcome.']],
'Do not delete influential points solely to improve significance or fit.','Inspect unexplained patterns and verify assumptions relevant to the claim.');
add('prediction','Prediction intervals, validation and overfitting',
'Predicting a new individual outcome is harder than estimating the mean outcome at an input. A prediction interval includes individual noise as well as estimation uncertainty. Model evaluation should use observations not used to fit or repeatedly choose the model.',
'Confidence interval for mean = uncertainty in an average response.|Prediction interval = uncertainty for a new outcome.|Overfitting = learning sample-specific patterns that do not generalize well.',
'Typical new-outcome uncertainty includes mean-estimation uncertainty plus residual variability',
'In a standard normal-error regression, prediction intervals are wider than mean-response confidence intervals at the same input and confidence level. Coverage depends on model assumptions and future similarity. Cross-validation should respect groups and time ordering; preprocessing belongs inside training folds. Repeated test-set selection turns the test set into validation data.',
[['Compare two intervals','An estimated mean response has interval [9,11].|A new-observation interval under the same fitted model may be [5,15].|The second includes individual variability.','Do not promise every outcome will lie in the mean interval.'],['Check memorization','Model A training error=0, validation error=10.|Model B training error=3, validation error=4.|B performs better on held-aside examples.','Lowest training error is not necessarily the best prediction procedure.'],['Respect time','Train on earlier observations and evaluate on later ones for a future-prediction task.|Fit scaling using training observations only.|Do not let future labels inform features.','Validation must imitate the information available in use.']],
'A successful validation score may not survive a changed future data distribution.','Separate uncertainty about a mean from uncertainty about an individual prediction.');
group='11 · Study design and evidence';
add('experiments','Randomized experiments and blocking',
'An experiment assigns conditions rather than merely observing them. Random assignment makes treatment groups comparable in expectation, supporting causal inference under design assumptions. It does not guarantee identical groups in a small realized sample or ensure the sample represents a broader population.',
'Treatment = assigned condition.|Random assignment = condition selected by a random procedure.|Block = a group of similar units within which assignment occurs.',
'Treatment effect estimate = outcome summary in treatment − outcome summary in control',
'Blocking can improve precision by balancing known sources of variation. Blinding can reduce behavior and measurement biases. Interference between participants, nonadherence, dropout and outcome switching complicate interpretation. Distinguish intention-to-treat comparisons of assignments from effects of treatment actually received. The design, not just the statistical test, carries causal justification.',
[['A randomized difference','Assigned-group means are 14 and 11.|Difference=14−11.|Estimated assignment effect=3 units.','Uncertainty and design limitations still need reporting.'],['Block before assignment','Units come from two sites with different baselines.|Randomize within each site.|Compare with an analysis respecting the blocking.','Blocking helps avoid chance imbalance across known sites.'],['Sampling is different','Volunteers are randomized to two methods.|Assignment supports an internal comparison.|Volunteer selection can still limit generalization to all people.','Random assignment and representative sampling answer different concerns.']],
'A randomized label does not remove problems from interference or selective dropout automatically.','Build a credible comparison into the study before collecting outcomes.');
add('ab-tests','A/B tests and sequential monitoring',
'An A/B test is an experiment comparing alternatives. Define the outcome, assignment unit, duration and decision rule before starting. Repeatedly checking a conventional fixed-horizon p-value and stopping as soon as it is small can inflate false positives.',
'Primary metric = the main preselected outcome.|Stopping rule = when and how a study ends.|Sequential method = a method designed for repeated looks under stated conditions.',
'Absolute lift = rate B−rate A; relative lift = (rate B−rate A)/rate A',
'Choose user-level or other valid randomization units to avoid dependence between repeated events. Account for seasonality, delayed outcomes, multiple metrics and unequal exposure. Sequential probability methods, alpha spending and other valid approaches require their own designs; ordinary fixed-sample formulas cannot be assumed safe under arbitrary optional stopping.',
[['Calculate lift','A rate is 10%, B rate is 12%.|Absolute lift=2 percentage points.|Relative lift=2/10=20%.','Both measures should name their units.'],['Avoid peeking bias','You check a fixed-horizon test daily and stop at the first p<0.05.|The procedure now includes many opportunities to stop.|Its nominal single-look error rate may no longer hold.','Use a prespecified horizon or a valid sequential procedure.'],['Choose the assignment unit','One user makes several visits.|Assigning independently by visit can mix experiences for the same user.|User-level assignment may better match the intervention.','Analysis should match assignment and dependence.']],
'Do not declare success solely from a favorable relative percentage with tiny absolute impact.','An A/B test needs a design, not just a dashboard p-value.');
add('causal','Causal inference, confounding and Simpson’s paradox',
'Causal questions ask what would happen if we changed something. Observational associations may instead reflect common causes, selection or different group compositions. Causal reasoning starts with a design and assumptions about how variables relate, not with an adjustment formula alone.',
'Confounder = a common cause relevant to exposure and outcome.|Counterfactual = an outcome under an alternative intervention.|Simpson’s paradox = an aggregate pattern differs from conditional group patterns.',
'Observed association is not generally equal to an intervention effect',
'Approaches such as matching, weighting, instrumental variables, regression discontinuity and difference-in-differences require distinct assumptions. Positivity, consistency and exchangeability appear in common causal frameworks. Controlling for a collider can create bias; controlling for a mediator changes the estimand. Clearly distinguish the effect of assignment, receipt, total effect and direct effect.',
[['A common cause','Hot days increase both ice-cream sales and swimming.|Swimming exposure can affect water-rescue counts.|Sales and rescue counts may correlate without sales causing rescues.','Consider shared causes before proposing an intervention.'],['A group-composition issue','One method is mostly used on harder cases.|Its overall success rate is lower.|Within comparable difficulty groups it may perform better.','Aggregate comparisons can reflect unequal case mix.'],['Difference-in-differences arithmetic','Treated outcome rises from 10 to 15; control rises from 8 to 10.|Changes are 5 and 2.|Difference in changes=3.','A causal interpretation needs assumptions such as parallel untreated trends, not just this subtraction.']],
'Adding every available variable to a regression is not a causal strategy.','State the intervention, comparison and identifying assumptions.');
add('surveys','Survey weights and finite populations',
'Surveys often sample people with unequal probabilities, group them geographically and adjust for nonresponse. Population estimates need to reflect this design. A weighted average describes a different balance from the unweighted average when inclusion probabilities differ.',
'Design weight = inverse selection probability.|Post-stratification = adjustment to known population group totals.|Finite-population correction = reduced sampling uncertainty when a substantial fraction is sampled without replacement.',
'Weighted mean=Σwᵢxᵢ/Σwᵢ; simple-random-sampling FPC=√[(N−n)/(N−1)]',
'The simple FPC assumes a known finite population and sampling without replacement under the corresponding design. Complex surveys often require strata, clusters and replicate weights for uncertainty calculations. Extreme weights can increase variance. Weighting to known totals cannot guarantee removal of bias from unmeasured nonresponse patterns.',
[['Compute a weighted mean','Values are 10 and 20 with weights 1 and 3.|Weighted total=10+60=70; weight sum=4.|Mean=17.5.','The second observation represents more weight in this target.'],['A finite-population correction','N=100 and n=50.|FPC=√(50/99).|Approximately 0.7107.','Under the specified design, sampling half the population reduces uncertainty relative to infinite-population approximation.'],['Extreme weights','One response has weight 100 while most have weight 1.|That response can dominate a summary.|Investigate design and sensitivity rather than silently deleting it.','A large nominal response count can hide a much smaller effective sample size.']],
'Applying weights to the mean but ignoring the design in standard errors can misstate uncertainty.','Survey estimation needs both weighted summaries and design-aware uncertainty.');
add('meta','Meta-analysis and combining evidence',
'Meta-analysis combines comparable estimates across studies. It should begin with a systematic question and inclusion process, not just available significant results. Differences in populations, outcomes and design can make combining studies inappropriate or require explicit modeling of heterogeneity.',
'Inverse-variance weight = greater weight for more precise estimates.|Common-effect model = studies estimate one shared effect under its assumptions.|Random-effects model = models a distribution of study effects.',
'Combined estimate=Σwᵢθᵢ/Σwᵢ; common-effect weights wᵢ=1/SEᵢ²',
'Random-effects weights incorporate between-study variance and require careful small-study inference. Heterogeneity, publication bias and dependence among estimates can undermine simple pooling. A precise combined number does not repair biased primary studies. Forest plots display study estimates and intervals; funnel asymmetry has multiple possible explanations.',
[['Combine two equally precise estimates','Estimates are 2 and 4, each with SE=1.|Weights are both 1.|Combined estimate=3.','This arithmetic assumes the specified common-effect structure and independent estimates.'],['Unequal precision','Estimates are 2 with SE=1 and 6 with SE=2.|Weights are 1 and 0.25.|Combined=(2+1.5)/1.25=2.8.','The more precise estimate contributes more weight.'],['Check comparability','One study measures a test score; another measures completion time.|Raw mean differences have different units.|They cannot be pooled directly as one raw difference.','Define a compatible effect measure before combining.']],
'Do not pool only published significant findings as if they were an unbiased sample of studies.','Combining evidence requires comparable targets, quality assessment and explicit assumptions.');
group='12 · Time, dependence and advanced methods';
add('time-series','Time series, trend and seasonality',
'A time series records values in order. Neighboring values are often related, so ordinary independent-row methods can fail. A trend is a longer-term movement; seasonality is a repeating calendar-related pattern. Start by plotting values against their actual times.',
'Trend = persistent direction or changing level.|Seasonality = a pattern repeating at a known period.|Autocorrelation = association between values at different time lags.',
'Difference at time t = yₜ−yₜ₋₁',
'Differencing can remove some changing levels, but can also amplify noise or overdifference a series. Stationarity describes stable distributional properties under time shifts in a specified sense, not simply a visually flat line. ARIMA, state-space and smoothing approaches make different assumptions. Missing timestamps and irregular spacing need explicit treatment.',
[['First differences','Values are [10,12,15,16].|Subtract adjacent values.|Differences=[2,3,1].','Differences describe changes, not levels.'],['A seasonal pattern','Every weekend has higher counts than weekdays.|The pattern repeats weekly.|Compare equivalent calendar positions when evaluating changes.','A weekend increase need not be a new trend.'],['Avoid independent-row thinking','Daily temperatures tend similar on adjacent days.|100 consecutive days are not necessarily 100 independent observations.|Uncertainty methods must account for dependence.','Order carries information in a time series.']],
'Randomly shuffling time before forecasting can leak future information.','Preserve time order and separate trend, seasonality and residual dependence.');
add('forecasting','Forecasting and rolling evaluation',
'A forecast predicts later observations using information available now. It must be evaluated in the direction of time. Simple baselines such as the last observed value or a seasonal repeat are useful comparisons before fitting complex models.',
'Forecast horizon = how far ahead the prediction looks.|Rolling origin = repeat training and testing while moving the forecast cutoff forward.|MAE = mean absolute prediction error.',
'MAE=Σ|actual−forecast|/n; moving average=mean of a specified recent window',
'Choose evaluation windows to reflect actual retraining and forecast horizons. Multi-step uncertainty can grow and predictions can be correlated. MAPE is problematic with zero or near-zero actual values and has other asymmetries. Report prediction intervals with their calibration checks, not only point-error scores.',
[['Last-value baseline','Observed values are 5,7,6.|A naive next forecast repeats the last value, 6.|If actual next value is 8, absolute error=2.','A baseline establishes what complexity must improve upon.'],['Moving average','Recent values are 4,6,8.|Their mean is 18/3=6.|Use 6 as a simple next-step forecast under this rule.','A moving average can lag a rising trend.'],['Evaluate errors','Actuals=[10,12], predictions=[9,15].|Absolute errors=[1,3].|MAE=(1+3)/2=2.','MAE is in the outcome’s original units.']],
'Never use future observations to fit a transformation for an earlier forecast.','Evaluate forecasting methods using realistic chronological cutoffs.');
add('survival','Time-to-event data and censoring',
'Sometimes the quantity is how long until an event happens. Observation may stop before the event occurs. That is censoring, not evidence that the event never happens. Survival methods use partial follow-up information without pretending every duration is fully observed.',
'Survival probability S(t) = chance the event time exceeds t.|Right censoring = event not observed before follow-up ends.|Hazard = an instantaneous event rate among those still at risk.',
'Kaplan–Meier estimate multiplies (1−events/at risk) at each event time',
'Kaplan–Meier estimation relies on suitable independent/noninformative censoring assumptions relative to the analysis. Cox proportional-hazards models compare hazard rates under a proportionality assumption; a hazard ratio is not a probability ratio. Competing risks require care because another event can prevent the event of interest.',
[['One event time','Five units are at risk and one event occurs.|Survival multiplier=1−1/5=0.8.|Estimated survival after that event time is 0.8.','The risk set determines the denominator.'],['A later event','Suppose four remain at risk and one later event occurs.|New multiplier=3/4.|Cumulative survival=0.8×0.75=0.6.','Survival is a product of conditional survival fractions.'],['Censored follow-up','A unit is observed event-free until time 10, then follow-up ends.|Its event time is known only to exceed 10.|Do not record 10 as an observed event duration.','Censoring retains partial information.']],
'Dropping all censored units can severely distort time-to-event summaries.','Account for incomplete follow-up instead of inventing event times.');
add('multivariate','Multivariate summaries, PCA and clustering',
'Multivariate data contain several measurements per observation. Their joint pattern may matter more than any single average. Covariance matrices summarize pairwise linear movement. PCA forms directions of variation, while clustering groups observations according to a chosen similarity.',
'Covariance matrix = table of variances and covariances.|PCA = projection onto ranked directions of centered variation.|Clustering = grouping without supplied class labels.',
'Covariance matrix diagonal = variances; off-diagonal entries = covariances',
'Feature scaling changes distances and PCA. A large-variance direction is not automatically the most predictive or meaningful direction. Cluster counts and algorithms encode choices; discovered groups need validation and context. Fit transformations using training data when evaluating a predictive workflow. Multivariate normality is stronger than each variable appearing normal separately.',
[['Read a covariance table','Matrix is [[4,1],[1,9]].|Variances are 4 and 9.|Standard deviations are 2 and 3; correlation=1/(2×3)=1/6.','The matrix combines individual spread and joint movement.'],['PCA variation share','Squared singular values are 9 and 1.|Total=10.|The first direction retains 9/10=90%.','Variation retained is not the same as predictive information retained.'],['Update a cluster center','One assigned cluster contains [1,3,5].|Its mean is 9/3.|The one-dimensional K-means center is 3.','The grouping rule and input scale determine the clusters.']],
'Do not give discovered clusters real-world labels without supporting evidence.','Joint structure needs careful scaling, assumptions and interpretation.');
add('advanced','Mixed models, robust methods and choosing a method',
'A broad statistics toolkit includes methods for grouped data, unusual distributions, measurement uncertainty and complex scientific designs. No one course can exhaust every specialization. Choose a method by the target question, observation unit, outcome type and dependence—not by whichever test name you remember.',
'Mixed model = fixed-effect structure combined with modeled random effects.|Robust method = a method designed to tolerate specified departures, not every problem.|Estimand = the precise quantity the analysis aims to learn.',
'Question → estimand → design → assumptions → method → diagnostics → interpretation',
'Mixed models can represent students within schools or repeated observations within people. Robust regression, quantile regression and robust standard errors solve different problems. Density estimation, nonparametric smoothing, spatial statistics, reliability, decision theory, missing-data models and measurement-error models extend the toolkit. Further study should include assumptions, simulations and diagnostic practice rather than only formula memorization.',
[['Grouped observations','100 pupils belong to 5 schools.|School-level influences can correlate their outcomes.|A suitable hierarchical model can represent this structure.','The effective information is not always equivalent to 100 independent pupils.'],['Mean or median target','Waiting times have a long upper tail.|A mean answers an average-total-cost question; a median answers a middle-experience question.|Select the target before selecting the estimator.','Different summaries may both be correct for different questions.'],['Choose a binary paired method','The same people answer yes/no before and after.|Outcomes are binary and paired.|A paired categorical method is more suitable than an independent two-mean test.','Design and outcome type narrow the method choice.']],
'A method called robust still has a specific target and assumptions.','Begin with the question and design; use calculations to support, not replace, reasoning.');
lessons.forEach((l,i)=>l.order=i+1);
const references=[
{title:'OpenIntro Statistics',url:'https://www.openintro.org/book/os/',note:'Further reading across introductory probability, inference and regression.'},
{title:'Introduction to Modern Statistics',url:'https://www.openintro.org/book/ims/',note:'Further reading on exploratory analysis, modeling and simulation-based inference.'},
{title:'NIST/SEMATECH statistical methods handbook',url:'https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/',note:'Further reading on statistical methods, assumptions and process analysis.'}
];
const data={lessons,references};if(typeof module!=='undefined'&&module.exports)module.exports=data;else root.STATS_DATA=data;
})(typeof window!=='undefined'?window:globalThis);
